# ── एक झलक — Environment Variables ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Copy this file to .env.local for local development.
# Production runs on Netlify, not Vercel: set these on the Netlify site under
# Site configuration → Environment variables.
#
# Everything listed here is read by code that exists in this repo. If you add a
# variable, add it here; if you delete the code, delete the entry.

# ── Site URL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used in metadata, sitemap, robots.txt and OG tags. Falls back to the
# production hostname when unset, so local builds work without it.
#   app/layout.tsx · app/sitemap.ts · app/robots.ts
# The apex, not www — www.ekjhalak.news 301s here. Unset in production, which
# means lib/site-url.ts's default is what actually runs.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://ekjhalak.news

# ── Groq (summarization + translation) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Rewrites over-long RSS bodies into a brief in the article's own language, and
# translates headline and brief into the other one, so every story is readable
# in both English and Nepali.
# Unset is fine: summaries fall back to a deterministic hard truncation and the
# UI shows every story in its original language.
# Free tier key at https://console.groq.com/keys
#   lib/summarizer.ts
GROQ_API_KEY=your-groq-api-key-here

# Optional, and usually better left unset.
#
# The free tier meters requests and tokens per model per day, so the
# summarizer defaults to openai/gpt-oss-120b and moves to the next name in the
# chain when a model 429s. Pinning a single name here replaces the default with
# one allowance. Accepts a comma-separated list, which is tried ahead of the
# built-in default.
# GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b

# Enrichment tuning. Defaults shown; all three are optional.
#   GROQ_BATCH_SIZE   stories per request (1–40)
#   GROQ_CONCURRENCY  requests in flight at once (1–8)
#   GROQ_BUDGET_MS    cap on the model stage, within AGGREGATE_BUDGET_MS
# GROQ_BATCH_SIZE=10
# GROQ_CONCURRENCY=3
# GROQ_BUDGET_MS=25000

# ── Regeneration deadline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The ceiling on one whole feed regeneration — RSS, article extraction and the
# model pass together.
#
# This matters more than it looks. getCachedFeed revalidates every 5 minutes and
# Next.js resolves that on the request path, so whichever reader arrives first
# after the entry goes stale pays for the entire pass. Netlify abandons a request
# at 30 seconds and returns 502, so a pass that overruns hands one reader every
# five minutes a broken page while everyone else sees a 0.3s cache hit — which is
# exactly as intermittent and as hard to reproduce as it sounds.
#
# GROQ_BUDGET_MS and EXTRACT_BUDGET_MS are caps *within* this, not allowances
# added to it, and every network call inside them clamps its own timeout to the
# time remaining. The pass reliably spends its whole budget, so this number is
# the cold-start latency a reader sees. Lower is safer, at the cost of how many
# stories each pass enriches; whatever a pass does not reach keeps the
# publisher's own text and is picked up by the next one.
#   lib/aggregator.ts
# AGGREGATE_BUDGET_MS=15000

# ── Article-page extraction ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Before a story is summarised — or dropped for having nothing to say — its own
# page is asked for body text (JSON-LD articleBody, then og:description, then
# the article's paragraphs). Where the page has more to say than the feed did,
# the page wins. No key needed; this is a plain HTTP fetch, cached per URL.
# A cap within AGGREGATE_BUDGET_MS, not an allowance added to it.
#   lib/article-extractor.ts
# EXTRACT_BUDGET_MS=15000

# ── Archive (Supabase) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every regeneration writes its stories to a Postgres table, so a story that
# falls out of the feeds is still on record — with when it was first seen, which
# outlets carried it, and what it said.
#
# Entirely optional. Unset, the site behaves exactly as it did before the table
# existed: /api/news builds from RSS and reads nothing from here. The write is
# bounded, awaited and swallows its own failures, so a database that is down or
# misconfigured costs the pass its 3-second reserve and nothing else.
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY bypasses row-level security. It is a server-only
# secret: never prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_, and never read it from a client
# component. The table has RLS enabled with no policies, so this key is the only
# thing that can write to it — which is the point, and also why it must not leak.
# Note this is the REST origin, not a Postgres connection string. The writer
# talks PostgREST over HTTPS, which is the right shape for serverless: stateless
# requests, no connection pool to exhaust. A direct Postgres connection on 5432
# is the wrong tool here — Netlify functions are ephemeral and would burn through
# the connection limit, and Supabase's direct-connect host is IPv6-only.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` is also read, because that is the name Supabase's
# own dashboard and every quickstart put in front of you. The origin is public by
# design — it is in the URL bar of every Supabase app — so reading either spelling
# is safe. SUPABASE_URL is preferred: it is the server-only name and the one the
# code documents. Check which one is in force with `pnpm check:archive`.
#
# The service-role key gets no such latitude. A NEXT_PUBLIC_ copy of it would be
# inlined into the browser bundle at build time and served to every visitor —
# lib/article-store.ts refuses to start the archive if it finds one, because that
# is a key to rotate, not rename.
#
#   lib/article-store.ts · lib/article-rows.ts
#   supabase/migrations/20260806000000_articles.sql
#   supabase/migrations/20260808000000_articles_enrichment.sql
# SUPABASE_URL=https://wfmurwsagrosxgcihbgw.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=  # Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API -> service_role
#
# Verify the whole thing — configuration, schema, RLS posture, row count:
#   pnpm check:archive
#
# And at runtime, authenticated with REVALIDATE_SECRET:
#   GET /api/archive?secret=…
# Reports whether this instance's last write succeeded, what PostgREST said if it
# did not, and a live row count. That endpoint exists because the archive was
# misconfigured for its entire first existence and wrote zero rows while every
# dashboard looked healthy — a warning in a log nobody tails is silence.

# ── Cache revalidation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POST /api/revalidate?secret=…  drops the news-feed cache tag.
# REQUIRED in production — the route returns 401 when this is unset and
# NODE_ENV is production, rather than silently accepting anyone.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#   app/api/revalidate/route.ts
# REVALIDATE_SECRET=your-long-random-secret-here

# Vercel sets this automatically for scheduled invocations and sends it as
# `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`. Set it in the dashboard to authenticate
# the daily cron defined in vercel.json.
#   app/api/revalidate/route.ts
# CRON_SECRET=your-long-random-secret-here

# ── Newsletter ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Double opt-in: the form emails a signed confirmation link, and the address is
# only added to the list when the reader clicks it. Nothing is stored in
# between — the pending signup travels inside the link.
#   app/api/subscribe/route.ts · app/api/subscribe/confirm/route.ts
#   lib/newsletter.ts · lib/subscribe-token.ts

# Signs and verifies the confirmation links. REQUIRED for double opt-in, and
# deliberately not defaulted: a hard-coded fallback would let anyone holding
# this code mint a confirmation for an address they do not own. Must be at
# least 16 characters. Without it the route falls back to single opt-in.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# NEWSLETTER_SECRET=your-long-random-secret-here

# ── Provider A: Resend (Vercel Marketplace) ───────────────────────────────────
# `vercel integration add resend/resend-email` sets RESEND_API_KEY for you.
# Resend's Audiences API is the subscriber list, so no database is needed, and
# it is the only provider that can send the confirmation email.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# A misspelled RESENT_API_KEY is also read, with a warning — the production key
# was stored under that name, which meant every signup since launch was answered
# with "the list is not open yet" and nothing was ever sent. Rename it and the
# fallback in lib/newsletter.ts can go.
# Audience is found or created by name on first use. Set this to pin a specific
# one instead.
# RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID=
# Must be on a domain verified in Resend, or every send is rejected.
# NEWSLETTER_FROM=EkJhalak <brief@ekjhalak.news>

# ── Provider B: any webhook ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any endpoint that accepts { "email": "…" } as JSON — Buttondown, ConvertKit,
# Formspree, a Zapier hook. A list endpoint is not a mail transport, so a
# deployment using only this falls back to single opt-in.
# Unset is fine and honest: the signup form tells readers the list is not open
# yet rather than faking a confirmation.
# NEWSLETTER_WEBHOOK_URL=https://api.example.com/subscribers
# NEWSLETTER_API_KEY=sent-as-a-bearer-token-if-set
